---
import CalcPage from '../../components/CalcPage.astro';
import Field from '../../components/Field.astro';
---

<CalcPage
  title="Ratio & proportion"
  kicker="Foundations"
  lede="The method most people meet first. It works, and you need to be able to read it because textbooks and lecturers use it — but it hides its mistakes, which is why the rest of this site leans on dimensional analysis instead."
  description="Solve a proportion by cross-multiplication, and see the same problem worked as a dimensional analysis chain."
>
  <form slot="form" id="calc">
    <fieldset>
      <legend>Label your units (optional, but it makes the setup readable)</legend>
      <div class="field-grid">
        <Field label="Left-hand unit" name="unitA" type="text" placeholder="mg" />
        <Field label="Right-hand unit" name="unitB" type="text" placeholder="mL" />
      </div>
    </fieldset>

    <fieldset>
      <legend>The relationship you know</legend>
      <div class="field-grid">
        <Field label="Amount" name="a" placeholder="250" />
        <Field label="corresponds to" name="b" placeholder="5" />
      </div>
    </fieldset>

    <fieldset>
      <legend>The relationship you want</legend>
      <div class="field-grid">
        <Field label="Amount" name="c" placeholder="400" />
        <Field label="corresponds to" name="d" placeholder="leave blank" />
      </div>
    </fieldset>

    <p class="hint" style="margin-top:-0.6rem">
      Leave exactly one of the four amounts empty. That empty box is what gets
      solved.
    </p>

    <div class="field-actions">
      <button class="btn btn--primary" type="submit">Solve</button>
      <button class="btn btn--quiet" type="reset">Clear</button>
      <button
        class="btn btn--quiet btn--tiny"
        type="button"
        data-example='{"unitA":"mg","unitB":"mL","a":"250","b":"5","c":"400","d":""}'
      >
        Example: stock solution
      </button>
      <button
        class="btn btn--quiet btn--tiny"
        type="button"
        data-example='{"unitA":"tablets","unitB":"mg","a":"3","b":"1500","c":"5","d":""}'
      >
        Example: tablets
      </button>
    </div>
  </form>

  <div slot="concept">
    <h2>What a proportion says</h2>
    <p>
      A ratio compares two quantities: <strong>250 mg per 5 mL</strong>. A
      proportion says two ratios describe the same relationship, so they stay
      equal as the numbers scale up or down.
    </p>

    <pre class="formula">  a       c
 ───  =  ───
  b       d</pre>

    <p>
      Because the fractions are equal, multiplying diagonally across the equals
      sign gives matching products. That is cross-multiplication:
    </p>

    <pre class="formula">a × d  =  b × c</pre>

    <p>Divide by whatever is attached to the unknown and you have your answer.</p>

    <h3>Where it goes wrong</h3>
    <p>
      Everything depends on the units sitting in matching positions. If
      milligrams are on top on the left, they must be on top on the right. Flip
      one side and the algebra still works perfectly — you will get a clean
      number, confidently wrong, with nothing in the working to warn you.
    </p>

    <p>
      That is the real problem with this method. It is not that it's difficult.
      It's that a setup error and a correct setup look identical once you start
      multiplying.
    </p>

    <h3>Why dimensional analysis is safer</h3>
    <p>
      <a href="/calculators/dimensional-analysis/">Dimensional analysis</a>
      solves the same problems by multiplying by conversion factors and letting
      units cancel. The advantage is that a wrong setup announces itself: the
      units come out as something impossible, and you catch the mistake before
      you ever reach an answer.
    </p>

    <p>
      Every worked solution here ends by showing the same problem both ways, so
      you can read it in whichever language you need.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
      <p>
        <strong>Sanity check regardless of method.</strong> If you asked for
        more drug, you should get more volume. If the answer moved the wrong
        direction, the setup was upside down.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</CalcPage>

<script>
  import { setupCalculator } from '../../scripts/calc.js';
  import { solveProportions } from '../../scripts/solvers/proportions.js';

  setupCalculator({
    form: '#calc',
    solve: solveProportions,
  });
</script>
